<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Košice Programme</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Košice</a> Programme was the government programme proclaimed on 5 April 1945 in Košice, in liberated eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, by the new Czechoslovak government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a>. Agreed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a>'s exiles and the communists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, it provided for alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, nationalisation, recognition of the Slovaks as a nation, and punishment and expulsion of Germans and Hungarians.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the final stages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a> of the Czechoslovak government-in-exile, which had been based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, negotiated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> leadership of the Czechoslovak Communist Party, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klement-gottwald">Klement Gottwald</a>, over the shape of a postwar government. The Communists, who had spent the war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, prepared the draft text. The parties of the London exile came to the talks in Moscow insufficiently prepared: apart from the Communists, only the Social Democrats put forward a proposal, and it was rejected by their own chairman, Zdeněk Fierlinger, a fellow traveller of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">Communism</a>.</p><p>A government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> and Slovaks was formed on 4 April 1945 with Fierlinger as prime minister. Officially, it approved the programme after arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Košice</a>, a city already taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. In fact the text had already been signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 29 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The programme ran to 16 chapters on the social, political and economic life of liberated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and on its international position. It described the new order as a people's democracy: a limited multi-party system resting on a government of national unity, in which every permitted party had to belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a>. This left no room for an opposition, and the Agrarian Party was banned.</p><p>It abandoned Czechoslovakism, recognised Slovak self-government with the Slovak National Council as its representative, and set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> and Slovaks on an equal footing. It restricted private ownership and provided for the nationalisation of large companies and other socialist reforms. German and Hungarian nationals were to be stripped of citizenship, with exceptions set by law, and the programme declared the collective guilt of these populations and of the right-wing parties for the breakup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and for cooperation with the Nazi regime. The army was to be rebuilt on the principles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, with propaganda officers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Called the programme of national and democratic revolution, the document turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>'s foreign, security and defence policy one-sidedly towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and in effect anchored its dependence on the USSR in law. It became the basis of the Third Czechoslovak Republic and, after the coup of 1948, of the Czechoslovak Socialist Republic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Košice Program” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A government-in-exile under Beneš in London was recognised by the Allies, and the <b>Košice Programme</b> of 1945 set the post-war order.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
